d struck that night: a break-in, a fire set to frighten, a thug with a message written in bruises. the seamstress took the worst of it and joked through split lips. Clara Whitfield wept and planned.\n\nThe witness lived in Victorian London's poorest court, old and afraid and tired of being afraid. a daguerreotype in Clara Whitfield's hands loosened their tongue: yes, they had seen. Yes, they would swear. No, they had never dared."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "the disgraced lord was taken — quietly, for the family's sake, but taken. The mill was sold. The debts were paid. The witness wept with relief, and the seamstress's bruises became badges.\n\nThe magistrate squirmed. The crowd — Victorian London's people, mill hands and merchants' wives — began to murmur, and the murmur became a voice. the disgraced lord's money couldn't buy this room. Not tonight.\n\nIt came to a head at the assembly rooms, in front of all Victorian London: Clara Whitfield stood and spoke, with a daguerreotype held high and the witness beside them. The gaslight seemed to lean in to listen."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Restoration', 'text': "Clara Whitfield went back to ordinary life, which turned out to be extraordinary enough: work, friends, Sundays, the small heroisms of kindness. a daguerreotype had taught them that history is just people, choosing.\n\nYears later, Clara Whitfield would show a daguerreotype to the curious and say: 'This is what courage looks like when it's old and paper.' And the young would listen, and Victorian London would remember.\n\nthe seamstress married the vicar's son — or daughter; Victorian London gossiped happily either way — and named their first child after Clara Whitfield, who pretended to mind and didn't."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Coda: The Drawer', 'text': "the past shapes us. In Victorian London, in every place, in every age. The end — which is to say, the beginning.\n\nThe gaslight is electric now. The carriages are cars. But a daguerreotype is still a daguerreotype, and Clara Whitfield is still Clara Whitfield, and the drawer still opens.\n\nthe seamstress visits with the children, who are told the story as a fairy tale — which, Clara Whitfield reflects, is what all true stories become, if they're lucky."}]}

